Association's Council of Faith

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.130
and Community Partnership,

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.260
and has served on the APA Council

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.474
of Minority Mental Health Disparities.

00:03:58.474 --> 00:04:01.210
He's someone who is just deeply
invested in the community

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.970
and also in integrating aspects
of mental health services

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.410
and faith communities as well.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.354
And something that I know again,

00:04:07.354 --> 00:04:08.380
that's gonna be just an important part

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:09.810
of today's conversation.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.730
So again, my apologies for just being

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.050
so overly enthusiastic,

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.660
but these are people who
have just been pioneers

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.700
in the field for so many different,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.210
in so many ways over the years.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.920
And especially as Black physicians,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.620
I just have to highlight
the excellence with

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.375
which they're doing the
work and the role models

00:04:25.375 --> 00:04:27.270
that they are as well.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.850
So again, you can tell I'm excited,

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:32.340
but I just had to be able to
give credit where credit is due

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.950
and grateful for both
of them, to both of them

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.710
for being here in this moment.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.230
So thanks again.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.714
- No, thank you Nii, thank
you for the opportunity

00:04:39.714 --> 00:04:40.641
and the space.

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:41.653
- Of course.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.540
Well, as my listeners know,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.400
I think it's always important
for us just to be able

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.869
to check in with each other
and see how we all are doing.

00:04:47.869 --> 00:04:50.980
So, even as you all have done
so many important things that

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:53.150
we celebrate, we also
do know that the work

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.460
that you do is critically important.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.470
And we know that there's a
backdrop of just challenges that

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:58.291
we've been facing in this society.

00:04:58.291 --> 00:05:00.300
We're recording this at the end of May.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.440
We've, you know, continued to experience

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.850
so many different shootings across

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:08.461
this country in Buffalo,
in California, in Texas

00:05:08.461 --> 00:05:10.410
at an elementary school.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.210
And we know that there are other
things that have been going

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.640
on that aren't even
highlighted in the media.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.890
And so we do take time to pause, you know,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.859
to be able to mourn and
to be able to reflect,

00:05:18.859 --> 00:05:21.107
and then also to call ourselves to action.

00:05:21.107 --> 00:05:23.560
But just in the context of all of that,

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.140
I just wanted to check in with both of you

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.610
and just see how you all are
doing at this point in time,

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.570
especially with the work
that you're already doing

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.229
within mental health and thinking

00:05:32.229 --> 00:05:34.500
about how we're processing
this as a society.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.193
So Ayana if we could start
with you, that'd be wonderful.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:42.381
- Yeah, I appreciate
you really centering all

00:05:42.381 --> 00:05:46.470
that has happened just in
the past couple of weeks

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.158
and really being very deliberate

00:05:49.158 --> 00:05:53.700
in checking in because
in so many of the spaces

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.790
that I have been in, I have
been the one checking in

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:01.840
with others because many
people just wanna go on

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.290
with business as usual.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:07.973
And I'm like, pause, literally
like pump the breaks.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.850
There is so much trauma right now,

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:16.260
and I have never ever, thank God really,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.130
I'm very grateful as by the grace of God,

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:23.070
I've never had a, you know,
serious mental illness that

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.780
I've had to really deal with,

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.020
but I can tell you psychologically,

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.920
it has been very difficult
for me to do my work

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.920
because I am constantly
thinking about safety.

00:06:38.331 --> 00:06:43.331
What places are safe,
you know, as a woman,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.460
as a Black woman, oftentimes
I think about safety in terms

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.020
of where do I park my car?

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:50.870
Where do I work?

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:55.870
Where do I live, but
extending that now to babies,

00:06:55.914 --> 00:06:59.373
my nieces and my nephew,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:03.952
worshiping like what
place is sacred, right?

00:07:03.952 --> 00:07:08.470
And so in a way that I
didn't really understand why

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:12.274
it impacted me so much until
I went to my own therapist.

00:07:12.274 --> 00:07:14.880
I'm in therapy every Tuesday at 11.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.680
I'm very clear about that
because I realize that

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.970
there's just not a sense of safety, right?

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.550
And so I have to grapple with that reality

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:31.550
and it's just been really difficult.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.260
And so I really appreciate
you checking in.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.300
And I, this to me was actually
restorative because I said,

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.740
I know that I'm going to be
in a space amongst people

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.190
that I trust to really talk through some

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:44.210
of these key issues,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.707
because none of us are
protected against feeling

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.840
the very real pain of what is happening.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.120
And so even if we're not in it directly,

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:00.813
this whole idea of vicarious
trauma is very real.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.360
And so I've just given myself grace.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:05.970
I've canceled meetings.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.690
I've said to my team
like, listen, take time.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.870
And if you're not okay,
I need you to be well,

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.290
because we can't do the
work of really trying

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.080
to liberate black folks. (laughs)

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.740
You know, that's the point of
Jordan Wellness Collaborative,

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.310
and really trying to liberate people

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:27.750
who have been totally
desecrated and just ignored

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.660
from society if you're not well.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:34.210
And yeah, I'm just mourning.

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.540
And I, you know, I checked in
with all of my loved ones just

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:38.410
to say, "I love you."

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:43.400
And just be very honest about
the reality of where we are in

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.770
this country and so,
yeah, it's been tough.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.034
It's been tough.

00:08:48.034 --> 00:08:50.440
And I appreciate you for checking in.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.467
- Oh, of course.

00:08:51.467 --> 00:08:53.070
I think it's so important,
to have that space.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:55.547
And I mean, in many ways,
you've like you've said,

00:08:55.547 --> 00:08:58.600
you're leading by example
because you're taking that time

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.000
for yourself and you're checking
in with those around you.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.013
And I know just with all those
who are looking up to you,

00:09:03.013 --> 00:09:05.344
I mean, I can speak
from personal experience

00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:07.370
and seeing trainees look up to you,

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.030
see you take that time to pause

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:10.967
and that in a sense gives them permission

00:09:10.967 --> 00:09:14.660
to also have that time
to pause, to have grace.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.920
And so definitely appreciate
the ways you continue to do

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.370
that, even though it's not easy.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:20.670
And the ways that you
lead by example as well.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:22.683
- Yeah.

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:25.623
- What about you?

00:09:25.623 --> 00:09:26.456
- Yeah, I'll say for me,

00:09:26.456 --> 00:09:29.373
it's just been emotionally exhausting.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:35.082
I think the Buffalo shooting
is especially poignant

00:09:35.082 --> 00:09:39.490
to our, you know, conversation tonight

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:43.120
because so many of the people
that were killed were leaders

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.650
in their church.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:49.263
A deacon, mothers, you know,
mothers of their churches.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.580
It was devastating.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.773
Elderly black folks murdered
in cold blood while shopping.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:02.917
So I mean, it just really hit home for all

00:10:02.917 --> 00:10:05.481
of the church members
and community members

00:10:05.481 --> 00:10:07.430
that we work with.

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:08.770
It made me think about my parents.

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:12.019
It made me think about
just all of my mentors.

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:14.324
And I think to Ayana's point,

00:10:14.324 --> 00:10:19.324
I've had a range of
emotions from rage to hatred

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.560
where this 18 year old who did this

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:31.560
and then published this manifesto
to just profound sadness,

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:35.910
to shock, to you know, you name it.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:40.910
And I think then Tuesday
happens, right, in Texas.

00:10:42.722 --> 00:10:45.680
And I think for me,

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:50.520
I have never felt as worried
for my kids as I did,

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.946
as I was reading those, the
articles about the story.

00:10:54.946 --> 00:10:58.731
I remember like literally
walking outta my apartment.

00:10:58.731 --> 00:11:02.110
It was my wife was bringing
our kids home from school

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:05.404
and I was just feverously
trying to look for them.

00:11:05.404 --> 00:11:08.500
And I know that was just
out of just the terror

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.190
that something could happen to them.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:14.210
And so it's just made me
feel tremendously vulnerable.

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:19.210
And also, you know, like
Ayana, I see a therapist.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:22.720
I saw her today at nine.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.063
So Thursday at nine is my session.

00:11:25.063 --> 00:11:29.097
And it's been amazing
to process it with her

00:11:29.097 --> 00:11:31.960
and also try to give the folks
in the community that we work

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:36.760
with time to process that,
cause it's been exhausting.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:40.140
- Yeah, so true.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.580
And again, just appreciate
your honesty because I think

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.960
in the same way as a leader,

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:49.400
others can see that, can hear
that and that can validate

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.000
those of all and just, you know,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.110
the range of emotions that come.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.850
And as I often share with people
sometimes multiple emotions

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:59.870
at once and just giving yourself
time to walk through that.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:02.220
And just to be honest about the challenges

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.370
that poses for all of us.

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:06.250
I didn't know if there was
another point you wanted.

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.150
- Yeah, I was just, I was listening

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.780
to what Sidney was
saying about in Buffalo.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:16.730
You know, so many of the
victims being involved

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.150
in their church, that being kind a,

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:20.600
really an important role in their lives

00:12:21.915 --> 00:12:23.150
and last Thursday,

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.002
so the Thursday after
the shooting on the 19th,

00:12:26.002 --> 00:12:29.810
I was at actually a community
event for Kathy Carroll,

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:33.283
who was one of my mentors
who passed away suddenly.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.886
And we needed someone to pray, to bless,

00:12:37.886 --> 00:12:41.127
raising money for a Kathy
Carroll Memorial Playground

00:12:41.127 --> 00:12:44.710
so that the babies have somewhere to play.

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.910
And so we needed someone
to bless the space.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:51.070
So I'm like, I know with
all these people out here,

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.950
we can find a praying
somebody, a pastor, a deacon,

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:54.783
somebody.

00:12:54.783 --> 00:12:59.783
So I'm walking around
to elderly black folks

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.150
and one of the guys said, "I'm a pastor."

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:04.490
And I said, "thank you, pastor.(laughs)

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:07.957
Can you, you know, bless the space?"

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:13.820
And before he opened it up for prayer,

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.510
I felt like very compelled
to obviously hold space

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.113
for the victims of Buffalo.

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:24.527
And he said to me,

00:13:24.527 --> 00:13:27.880
"I am so happy that you did that because

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.253
my cousin was murdered.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.700
He was one of the victims.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:34.533
- Wow.

00:13:34.533 --> 00:13:37.600
- And I have just been
going and going and dealing

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.800
with the funeral plans and
he's a pastor, you know?

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:42.633
And he's like,

00:13:42.633 --> 00:13:44.821
"this is the first time
that I've been able to like,

00:13:44.821 --> 00:13:49.821
just take a breath and realize
like, this is really real."

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:55.170
And he got so emotional.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:14:00.170
I got so emotional because here
I am, I was in Connecticut.

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:05.880
You know, I wasn't even thinking
that like I would run into,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:07.150
I was just holding his space

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:11.020
because I've somehow felt
connected just for the trauma

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:14.400
of being Black and these people shopping.

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.440
But then it became very real,

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.810
like this is this man's
cousin, you know what I mean?

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.510
Like we are all interconnected.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:25.760
And so that was really heavy, man.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:26.593
You know what I mean?

00:14:26.593 --> 00:14:31.480
Like this is not something
to do because it looks good

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:32.313
or sounds good.

00:14:32.313 --> 00:14:34.493
Like a check-in is real, you know?

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.010
And I find myself seeing that we

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:43.380
as Black people are often checking in

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.440
and it's like do we do that because

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:47.830
we're mental health
professionals, we're trained

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:51.410
or do we do that because
we understand that we need

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.733
to see one another in a real way?

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:57.190
And for me, I think it's the latter,

00:14:57.190 --> 00:15:01.904
'cause I've been in many
spaces where I being

00:15:01.904 --> 00:15:06.904
the Black person has had to say, you know.

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:11.130
And so I just think that
speaks to a lot of what help

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:14.380
we get into today is our
collective resilience.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:19.380
But also when is it going to
be our turn for others to check

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:20.610
in with us?

00:15:25.053 --> 00:15:26.665
- Well said.

00:15:26.665 --> 00:15:27.550
When will others check in on us?

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:28.383
Yeah.

00:15:29.674 --> 00:15:30.800
- Yeah.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:32.650
I mean to that question,
have you have either

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:34.430
of you seen a shift in that at all?

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:39.430
Does it still seem like
people are not fully aware?

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:42.637
Maybe it might not be
the best way to say it,

00:15:42.637 --> 00:15:44.296
but just curious if you wanna elaborate

00:15:44.296 --> 00:15:47.953
on your experience in that realm as well.

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:56.680
- I mean, I think that it can
be difficult for white people

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:03.150
to bring up issues.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:07.800
They don't, I think it's
hard for them to initiate.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:08.657
Some of my white friends have said,

00:16:08.657 --> 00:16:11.540
"I didn't know how to say,
I wanted to say something.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:12.707
I didn't know how."

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:16.730
And then, so there's that camp.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:20.830
And then there's also some
folks that are just kind

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:25.170
of oblivious and just to Ayana's point,

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.640
just kind of going on business as usual.

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:32.110
So I think it, I've seen
kind of both extremes

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:37.110
and I do think it's
critical that we do pause,

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:40.420
but we also, and, you know,

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:44.070
give space to our white
colleagues to help them understand

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:48.280
that they need to be the ones
that initiate that instead

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.373
of always being Black folks.

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:52.483
- Yeah.

00:16:52.483 --> 00:16:54.200
- Yeah, I think Sidney's right on point

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.507
because so Mondays I have lab meetings.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.090
I try not to call my lab a lab.

00:17:02.090 --> 00:17:03.480
It's the Jordan Wellness Collaborative,

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.410
but for you all, I call it
lab, but we're in a community.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:07.570
It's not a lab 'cause of stigma.

00:17:07.570 --> 00:17:08.570
Y'all got it.

00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:10.650
But Mondays I have meetings with the team

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:15.420
and this was right after the shooting,

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:17.330
the Monday right after the shooting.

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:22.330
And I was lit, like I like
was literally sobbing.

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:25.997
I was literally sobbing because
just before the meeting.

00:17:25.997 --> 00:17:30.997
I saw all the pictures of the
victims and like Syd said,

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:34.780
I was, Sidney was
saying, I was like, whoa,

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.240
this could have been my mom, my uncle.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:41.280
Like I saw myself in so
many of these young people.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:42.560
I mean these people,

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.560
but then I started to think
about the young people,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:46.835
'cause I read about a
eight year old that hid

00:17:46.835 --> 00:17:51.835
in the cooler so that
she wouldn't get shot.

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:56.293
That's the same age as my niece.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:02.840
And so it, I just began to
sob in collective mourning

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:05.040
because it's like, how
much more can we take?

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:08.150
You already shot up
our churches, you know.

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:11.060
Any who, so I was sobbing

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:14.780
and people didn't know what was going on.

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:16.917
And I took a breath and
I said, "I'm going to,

00:18:16.917 --> 00:18:19.020
we're not having this meeting.

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:21.050
Clearly I'm not in a place
to have this meeting,

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:24.660
but I am going to have
you all see the pictures

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.140
because this is someone's mother.

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:29.883
This is someone's aunt,
brother, sister, et cetera."

00:18:29.883 --> 00:18:32.597
And a white woman that's
a part of my team.

00:18:32.597 --> 00:18:34.518
She said, Ayana,

00:18:34.518 --> 00:18:37.660
"thank you so much for doing this

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:40.450
because it didn't even hit me."

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:43.070
She's like "I heard about it."

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:45.116
But she's like, "you
know, I live in the suburb

00:18:45.116 --> 00:18:48.720
around predominantly white people.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.690
Like I read it and
thought, oh, that's that

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:51.980
and kept moving."

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:55.373
She said, "but not until
you showed the pictures,

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.740
said their names."

00:18:57.740 --> 00:18:59.590
I said a little bit about them.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:02.516
Like one, you know, was
a deacon in the church.

00:19:02.516 --> 00:19:07.470
Just affirm their humanity
that she understand.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:08.807
And she's like,

00:19:08.807 --> 00:19:12.190
"I appreciate you doing
this because that helped me

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:15.090
to have more empathy and to pause."

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:16.870
But see it's the thing is like,

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:20.550
why do we have to show you that we matter.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.730
And the way that these Latin brothers

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:28.730
and sisters were killed in
Texas, I didn't have to like,

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:31.666
like I was still affected by that.

00:19:31.666 --> 00:19:34.080
I don't know them.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:35.950
But it, see that's my humanity

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:37.960
because I know what it is, right.

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:41.370
But it's like, I just am so torn Sidney,

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:42.420
'cause Tiffany's right.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:47.420
It's like we have to give our
white colleagues the space

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:50.190
to get it, but then I'm like,

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.010
but you should get it
because these are people.

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:54.350
But then I understand
that this is a collective

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:57.958
of them not valuing our lives, right.

00:19:57.958 --> 00:20:00.410
- That's the message.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:03.020
- Yeah and so important, like you said,

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:05.010
to bring that level of humanity.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:07.940
And also, I've just seen unfortunately

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:10.737
because of how things have
been going the last few years,

00:20:10.737 --> 00:20:13.530
people have even talking
about the opportunities

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:16.310
for us to unite as people, but different,

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:18.150
even different communities of color.

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:21.710
So as we had, you know, Rabbi
Buchdahl on the podcast,

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:23.750
a couple or the last episode,

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:25.130
talking about the hostage situation

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:28.060
that she helped navigate
at a synagogue in Texas

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:31.620
and all the things that she
could relate to on that front.

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:34.810
And then also the people of
color in her Jewish communities

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:37.460
who are also sharing about
their experiences, you know,

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:38.420
from mixed families,

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:40.570
Blacks who are also Jewish

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:41.920
and the things that they have felt,

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:43.760
which we can relate to as well

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.370
or when Juju Chang was talking
about all the anti-Asian hate

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:48.200
and how people had checked in with her.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:49.910
But she hadn't necessarily
thought about checking in with

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:52.140
her African American
colleagues, same type of thing.

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:54.540
But then there was so much
that she could relate to.

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.420
So even in those circles, but again,

00:20:56.420 --> 00:20:58.280
it's like you were saying,
even in those communities,

00:20:58.280 --> 00:20:59.790
people can relate to the experience

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:01.100
and they're having easier time I think,

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:03.580
seeing a joint humanity.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:05.680
But then when we, you know,
when we're talking about many of

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:06.810
our white brothers and sisters,

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:09.270
it's a different type of engagement.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.720
So, Ayana like you
mentioned it's necessary,

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.170
but it's also challenging
because you don't want

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:15.920
to be the one to have to
bring that all the time.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:16.980
And so I think, you know,

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:20.259
it's just something that's
important to be able

00:21:20.259 --> 00:21:21.980
to hold that space and again,

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:24.460
just the way that you
have done that by example,

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:26.280
and being able to pull people in,

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.050
but then that can be exhausting
in and of itself, so.

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:29.883
- It is.

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:32.537
- Let me tell you something.

00:21:32.537 --> 00:21:34.300
I tell my mentees this,

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:36.910
and it's something that like,
you know, sometimes it's like,

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:39.320
you gotta practice what you
preach and I haven't done that,

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.050
but I've honestly learned from Sidney

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:44.310
and I will shout him out
because one time I was,

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:45.150
I don't know something.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:48.170
And he's like, "I am not
available, da, da, da,

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:50.196
I am my kids are," and I was like, listen,

00:21:50.196 --> 00:21:55.196
like that to me was so
gangsta because I'm like, yes,

00:21:56.170 --> 00:22:00.050
he is protecting him, access to him.

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:03.500
And then I had to learn
why am I not doing that?

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:08.350
Because for me, it's like,
oh no, I'm socialized to,

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:10.917
for people always to have
access to me, do this, do that.

00:22:10.917 --> 00:22:14.910
But it's like, no, you
can protect your space.

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:16.866
So anyway, I tell my mentees

00:22:16.866 --> 00:22:19.130
and I don't know where this came from,

00:22:19.130 --> 00:22:22.361
but rest, resist, repeat.

00:22:22.361 --> 00:22:25.963
Rest, resist, repeat.

00:22:27.445 --> 00:22:28.600
And I am at my resting.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:32.320
So on Monday I was like, I am out,

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:35.810
don't call me, don't
text me, don't email me.

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:40.770
Cause it is not getting so
I'm at a point where like,

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:43.591
even this weekend, like I
love my family, you know,

00:22:43.591 --> 00:22:46.940
Black people, we love our
families for the most part,

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:49.188
but I was like, y'all can't stress me.

00:22:49.188 --> 00:22:50.367
(Sidney laughing)

00:22:50.367 --> 00:22:52.340
- I was supposed to
have the whole gang here

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:56.280
for the long holiday weekend.

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:58.610
And I was feeling just
so emotionally exhausted

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:00.150
from everything that we're talking about.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:02.704
And I was like, I love
y'all, but Aunt Ayana,

00:23:02.704 --> 00:23:07.230
the Connecticut home is
not happening, you know,

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:10.210
because I'm in this phase of rest

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:12.660
and I'm realizing that I have to do that

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:16.483
to protect my peace.

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:21.370
For all the Addy listeners in the podcast,

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:25.190
you guys are getting the what's it called,

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:30.070
exclusive 'cause I'm talking
to the y'all residents

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.020
for graduation.

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:33.570
And I was thinking about
what am I gonna say to them,

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:36.300
to and try to inspire
them during this time.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.660
And the message that kept coming

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:40.070
to me was protect your peace.

00:23:40.070 --> 00:23:43.030
So that's, I'm in a state
of rest and I will resist

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:46.950
because I really do think
that my identity in itself

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:48.460
is an act of political warfare.

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:49.800
So I have to resist.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:52.500
But right now I'm in a period of rest

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:56.540
and Sidney showed me how
to do that and modeled.

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:58.270
And so I think that's important in terms

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:00.230
of the collective community.

00:24:00.230 --> 00:24:02.670
You don't even realize
sometimes you're just moving.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:05.010
How do you move in a
way and you don't know

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:07.370
that you have people looking at you?

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:09.060
So it's like, absolutely.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.530
And so I'm so grateful.

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:13.660
So then Nii, you say, "Ayana,
can you do the podcast?"

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:14.493
Yes.

00:24:14.493 --> 00:24:16.270
Now I don't know when I'm
gonna do it, but you know,

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:17.685
we're here.

00:24:17.685 --> 00:24:19.010
I'm gonna do it because I believe in you,

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:20.270
brother, Black man.

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:22.507
I was so happy when I
got to Yale, I saw Nii.

00:24:22.507 --> 00:24:26.410
I was like, who is this fine man?

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:28.210
Don't cuss me out to your wife,

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:33.210
who is professor at Yale?
(Syndey laughs)

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:35.330
I mean, I was so inspired.

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:36.310
I was a resident.

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:37.490
I was so happy.

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:41.190
You were an assistant professor
existing in that space.

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:42.750
And I was like, oh, this is amazing.

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:46.500
So I think that anyway,
it's just so important.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:50.693
I'm so happy because it's
like we have to survive

00:24:50.693 --> 00:24:54.610
because sometimes we don't even
realize who is watching us,

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:59.390
who we are impacting
by just modeling that.

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:00.940
And so I'm so grateful.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:02.930
Like I truly, I really mean this

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:03.810
and I have to tell Sidney,

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:05.100
'cause I don't know if I already told him,

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:08.660
like I reached out to him years
ago when I was trying to do

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:13.330
my cade for the Black
Church Project to have,

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:14.460
and I don't wanna get emotional,

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:16.390
but I was like trying to figure this out

00:25:16.390 --> 00:25:19.770
because so many people kept
saying, "you can't do this.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.867
You cannot provide addiction treatment."

00:25:22.852 --> 00:25:26.010
I have reached out to a PO and the NIH

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:30.400
and they just told me
like, "you can't do it."

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.300
You know?

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:34.020
And so I was just on the
strength of reaching out to him,

00:25:34.020 --> 00:25:35.840
reading his papers and the space.

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:39.177
I'm like he modeled it and depression.

00:25:39.177 --> 00:25:41.163
And like he was so helpful.

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:45.250
He would not only was he like,
"absolutely you can do this,"

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:46.667
but he's like, "what do you need?"

00:25:46.667 --> 00:25:48.990
"You need my grant, you
need this, you need that."

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:49.823
It wasn't not like who are you?

00:25:49.823 --> 00:25:51.000
He didn't know me.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:52.310
I was nobody.

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:54.010
Like now I got a little piece of the pie,

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:54.940
but back then.

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:59.323
- You got a big piece
of the pie. (laughing)

00:25:59.323 --> 00:26:00.870
- It's so important.

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:04.410
So it, for me, it comes full circle

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:07.640
because it's like we have to,

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:10.670
we don't have to, but I feel
I'll speak from a place that

00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:14.989
I feel a collective responsibility
sometimes to be there

00:26:14.989 --> 00:26:19.989
for investigators,
especially who are Black.

00:26:20.920 --> 00:26:23.730
And in order to do that,
I am seeing more and more.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.239
I have to protect my peace.

00:26:25.239 --> 00:26:28.560
I have to have boundaries around me.

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:30.352
I have to have team members,

00:26:30.352 --> 00:26:33.430
white people included that are going

00:26:33.430 --> 00:26:35.740
to understand the importance of the work

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.360
that I'm gonna have to
model sometimes empathy,

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:40.973
how to build that empathy.

00:26:42.830 --> 00:26:46.730
In order to be whole, I
need to take care of myself.

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:50.360
So that's why I have,
I get on good vacations

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:51.550
and I treat myself well.

00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:54.470
And when I live in a
Harlem, my apartment is fly.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:55.303
You know what I mean?

00:26:55.303 --> 00:26:58.100
'Cause I have to protect my peace, yeah.

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:01.070
- Yeah, so well said.

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:03.180
I mean so many important pieces there too.

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:06.080
And like you said, I mean the
representation matters even

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:07.880
as you're trying to get,

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:09.360
as you were trying to get into the work

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:10.217
and get into the space.

00:27:10.217 --> 00:27:12.790
I mean, I actually want to lean
into that a little bit too,

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:14.360
'cause as you talked about
even the way you said

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:17.090
you don't talk about lab
meeting in different settings,

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:18.630
'cause that stigma still is there,

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:21.310
especially when we think
about in our churches.

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:22.310
So, you know, for the listeners,

00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:25.353
how did you all actually
create that space?

00:27:25.353 --> 00:27:28.570
Because in the sense
you're leading by example,

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:33.290
but you also bring all that
institutional background

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:35.310
with you, for good or for bad.

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:39.750
And so how did you build that
trust to even have this kind

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:44.750
of integrated mental health
investment in faith communities

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:46.460
to even get that going?

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:48.200
Cause there's so many not to be,

00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:49.810
there's so many areas where I feel like

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:51.930
that could get easily derailed,

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:53.480
but you've both done that with excellence.

00:27:53.480 --> 00:27:55.570
So you, I'm just, if you
can share that, you know,

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:56.860
with our listeners as well.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:57.920
- Sure, I'll go.

00:27:57.920 --> 00:28:01.640
And then because I, and
then I think what Ayana did

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.040
is a hundred times more difficult

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:08.510
than what I did just because of,

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:10.570
because in the Black church,

00:28:10.570 --> 00:28:13.260
at least growing up in the south,

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:16.800
there was more stigma I
think around substance use

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:19.250
and around like depression.

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:21.390
And so that's why I say what you did

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:25.001
I think was in many
respects more challenging

00:28:25.001 --> 00:28:26.513
because of that.

00:28:27.407 --> 00:28:30.810
You know, I kind of came
into this work, you know,

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.620
I grew up in the church, you know.

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:35.350
My dad's a deacon at Shiloh
Baptist Church, new site.

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:37.763
My mama played the piano
for the little kids choir

00:28:37.763 --> 00:28:39.600
that we sang in.

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:42.790
So I was at choir rehearsal on Thursdays

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:44.473
and in service on Sundays.

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:48.140
Vacation Bible school during the summer.

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:51.040
Some of my best friends
are from the church,

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:54.863
my college roommate, just my
brother who went to Hampton.

00:28:55.801 --> 00:28:56.801
- Shout out!

00:28:57.895 --> 00:28:59.191
Shout out to the real HU!

00:28:59.191 --> 00:29:00.631
I didn't know that.

00:29:00.631 --> 00:29:02.882
(all laughing)

00:29:02.882 --> 00:29:03.715
Ok.

00:29:03.715 --> 00:29:04.548
- That's right.

00:29:04.548 --> 00:29:09.548
So, and then so church provided a big part

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:11.460
of just community.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.730
It grounded me in my blackness and it,

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:17.590
you know, provided the
foundation of my spiritual life.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:22.590
And so I remember coming
up to New York in 2009

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.040
for a research fellowship
and knew that I wanted

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:27.870
to do something to help our people.

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:32.270
And I gave a talk at Greater
Allen AME Cathedral in Queens

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:34.013
about stigma and mental health.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:37.035
And it was really well received

00:29:37.035 --> 00:29:40.980
and that kind of developed a partnership

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:43.340
with the health ministry leader.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:45.430
And we got a small grant,

00:29:45.430 --> 00:29:47.520
a NARSAD Young Investigator Award.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:51.288
And that was kind of my
entree into this space.

00:29:51.288 --> 00:29:56.288
And so I think that it
really took just, you know,

00:29:56.362 --> 00:29:59.100
we're a communal people, right?

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:01.740
So it just took being present.

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:03.070
It took being vulnerable.

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:08.070
It took, people had to know
that this was like my ministry

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:09.403
and not just my job.

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:14.120
You know, and I think to Ayana's point

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:15.760
about political warfare,

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:17.264
I completely agree with that.

00:30:17.264 --> 00:30:21.610
And we really framed this
as a issue of social justice

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:24.150
in spiritual warfare.

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:26.128
And I think that resonated with folks.

00:30:26.128 --> 00:30:27.950
- Wow.

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:31.440
- So that's kind of how
we, you know, started.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.520
We did focus groups with pastors,

00:30:33.520 --> 00:30:37.860
then we did depression screening
surveys with the PHQ-9.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:38.693
Then we created

00:30:38.693 --> 00:30:40.924
this community health
working training program,

00:30:40.924 --> 00:30:43.670
recruiting folks from Black churches.

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:47.890
And that led to, you know,
our current, you know,

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:49.730
some of our current grants now.

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:52.310
So it was really one
church at a time building

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:54.913
those relationships, going
to ministry meetings,

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:58.280
doing a lot of presentations,

00:30:58.280 --> 00:30:59.800
making a lot of referrals for people.

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:02.760
Cause I think folks had to
know that you're there for more

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:05.980
than just a grant or a
paper cause folks was tired

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:08.420
of the helicopter research, right.

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:10.260
They tired of folks
coming in doing something

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:13.030
and then leaving and not
giving anything back.

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:15.050
I think that it really took that trust

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:18.910
and being able to call out
institutionalized racism,

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:22.200
calling out psychiatric
misdiagnosis among Black men,

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:27.149
calling out, you know,
over criminalization

00:31:27.149 --> 00:31:31.282
of black folks during the
cocaine epidemic and then,

00:31:31.282 --> 00:31:33.134
you know, with opioids.

00:31:33.134 --> 00:31:36.440
So being able to talk about
all of those social, cultural

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:40.500
and historical issues,
I think was critical.

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:41.744
- Yeah.

00:31:41.744 --> 00:31:43.302
I mean just hearing you speak, I mean,

00:31:43.302 --> 00:31:45.080
it sounds like you just
brought your whole self,

00:31:45.080 --> 00:31:46.350
- Yes.
into those situations.

00:31:46.350 --> 00:31:47.183
You were honest,

00:31:47.183 --> 00:31:49.570
you were real about the
institutional challenges.

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:51.320
You were honest about the need.

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:53.270
You were building the trust.

00:31:53.270 --> 00:31:54.610
So I mean, I would just get the sense

00:31:54.610 --> 00:31:56.440
that people knew where
you were coming from.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:57.317
They knew it was a place of sincerity.

00:31:57.317 --> 00:31:59.710
It was a place where
you were trying to help.

00:31:59.710 --> 00:32:01.831
And I imagine you, there probably
still was some hesitation

00:32:01.831 --> 00:32:04.360
in some places, but I'm sure,
it sounds like you just,

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:05.983
you worked through that and
you addressed the big issues

00:32:05.983 --> 00:32:08.190
that had to be addressed as well.

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:09.580
- Yeah and I think the key thing is,

00:32:09.580 --> 00:32:10.690
and Ayana does this too,

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:13.370
is that just the community
based participatory approach.

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:17.534
Right, just having folks
from the community leading,

00:32:17.534 --> 00:32:20.490
elevating community voices

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:24.490
and trusting them that
they know the answers

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:27.270
and the solutions and
just trusting the process.

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:30.390
And being a vessel to bring
their vision to light.

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:32.572
So I think that was just critical,

00:32:32.572 --> 00:32:34.610
absolutely critical.

00:32:34.610 --> 00:32:35.890
- Yeah, I appreciate you saying that.

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:37.810
I hope that resonates
with the listeners too,

00:32:37.810 --> 00:32:39.510
just 'cause people who
are trying to wrestle

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:41.300
with the best way to kind
of approach these things,

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:43.270
just as you mentioned
elevate their voices.

00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:44.103
- Right.

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:47.013
- Yeah and I think I, you know,

00:32:47.013 --> 00:32:50.930
I appreciate the kind of
arc that Sidney presented,

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:55.930
but I also wanna just not
allow him to minimize kind

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:00.360
of the impact of the space, right.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:02.270
Because it's really
thinking about like Jay-Z

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:03.103
and the "Blueprint."

00:33:03.103 --> 00:33:04.440
First of all, I love hip hop.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:07.240
So I always gonna think about
how to bring in hip hop.

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:09.850
Shout out to Kendrick Lamar's
album is so good right now.

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:12.720
But anyway, thinking about the "Blueprint"

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:17.640
because Sidney had already, for me anyway,

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:22.020
published in this area when
I had so many naysayers,

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:27.020
honestly, both from
academia, right, NIH, but,

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:30.980
and also people in the
church, Black people.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:33.950
They were like, "you can't,
we ain't talking about"

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:37.010
One pastor was like, "I don't
got no addicts in my church.

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:38.020
What you trying to say?

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:39.890
I got addicts in my church."

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:42.520
And I was like, first of all,
I don't use that language,

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:43.384
but okay.

00:33:43.384 --> 00:33:44.217
(both laughing)

00:33:44.217 --> 00:33:46.640
But also I'm like just showing the stats.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:51.603
I'm like people who misuse
substances are everywhere.

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:55.750
And I'm not saying that this is just you

00:33:55.750 --> 00:33:58.510
or a reflection of you pastoring or not,

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:00.749
but I'm just anyway.

00:34:00.749 --> 00:34:01.675
(both laughing)

00:34:01.675 --> 00:34:06.675
But having to come in with
actual data to say like,

00:34:09.090 --> 00:34:14.090
look here is a Black man
who work with Black men

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:17.840
in the church and you know,

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:20.980
really was able to
address some of the things

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:23.600
that may not be as heavily stigmatized

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:25.310
as having a substance use disorder,

00:34:25.310 --> 00:34:27.160
but ain't nobody talking about depression.

00:34:27.160 --> 00:34:29.950
Like that really just came
within the last decade,

00:34:29.950 --> 00:34:32.340
really heavy in the last five years.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:36.090
So Sidney was doing this
work before then, right?

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:40.220
So having that like blueprint
truly to be able to say like,

00:34:40.220 --> 00:34:42.230
look, somebody has already done it in

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:45.000
this behavioral health space.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:49.940
We can use a lot of the
work to already show what

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:52.790
is the most important, being
able to partner with community

00:34:52.790 --> 00:34:54.103
to get buy in.

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:59.000
Similarly, I was very
comfortable in the church 'cause

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:01.210
I was raised in a Black Baptist church.

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:06.210
So I knew how to first
giving all glory to God.

00:35:06.323 --> 00:35:07.156
(Sidney laughs)

00:35:07.156 --> 00:35:07.995
- All glory to God.

00:35:07.995 --> 00:35:11.180
- It was just like that is who I am.

00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:15.620
They saw, this is important,
but I'm not just here to,

00:35:15.620 --> 00:35:20.620
you know, advance in my
career and say deuces.

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:26.050
Like I literally joined
Varick even before the pastor

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:29.410
who is here now, Pastor
Steele, like anyway,

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:30.930
I was there right?

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:32.750
When we were looking for LatinX churches

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:35.180
to think about how do we
provide telehealth medication

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:36.350
for addiction treatment?

00:35:36.350 --> 00:35:38.390
I like went out to the church,

00:35:38.390 --> 00:35:41.240
figuring out like I know
I can't be the vessel.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:44.770
I need to really work
with my LatinX brothers

00:35:44.770 --> 00:35:47.860
and sisters who are actively
using drugs and say,

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:49.370
can you help me figure out,

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:51.931
like what are the churches that are cool,

00:35:51.931 --> 00:35:53.770
that you feel safe with?

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:55.810
Cause every church ain't a church

00:35:55.810 --> 00:35:59.330
that the pastor's gonna be
okay with having addicts.

00:35:59.330 --> 00:36:00.820
I would never say that, but you know,

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:03.680
that's what people say, come
into the church for care.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:05.039
- That's right.

00:36:05.039 --> 00:36:07.200
- So really elevating those
voices in the community

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:11.580
and they showing me and
putting, can we talk about this?

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:12.413
Okay.

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:18.270
Me, these institutions don't
wanna pay community partners.

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:21.960
And so my thing is,
how can I use mine now?

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:26.410
I do have a little bit of power
to say to a place like NYU

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:29.140
or say to a place to
Yale, you gotta pay people

00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:32.070
and not pay 'em for no
community advisory board,

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:34.060
pay them so they can pay their bills.

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:34.893
- [Sidney] That's right.

00:36:34.893 --> 00:36:36.540
- Because what Sidney was
saying now we're thinking

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:39.310
about social justice is not just the tool

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:42.330
of accessing in my case,
substance use treatment

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:44.190
in the church, which
has been revolutionary,

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:46.510
and I'm super excited
because we're showing

00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:48.730
that people are showing
up in the churches more

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:50.349
than they are traditional
addiction clinics

00:36:50.349 --> 00:36:52.803
and that's just facts.

00:36:52.803 --> 00:36:56.911
But also saying that for
people who are working

00:36:56.911 --> 00:36:59.640
as community investigators,

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:02.610
I really think about them
as CO-Is on the project.

00:37:02.610 --> 00:37:06.238
How to then do we set up a blueprint again

00:37:06.238 --> 00:37:10.450
for economic security?

00:37:10.450 --> 00:37:14.280
So that you are not just
worried about getting $500

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:15.740
for a community advisory board,

00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:17.960
but this is a role you have expertise,

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:20.570
you can put it on your CV,

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:23.550
trainings you can get
so that you can advance

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:27.040
your own career so that you
can really support your family.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:29.030
Right, so now when people are thinking,

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:31.240
how do they work with the
Jordan Wellness Collaborative

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:33.680
for community programs
and faith-based settings,

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:38.310
I'm like, how can I hire you
through the institution so

00:37:38.310 --> 00:37:39.830
you can have benefits.

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:41.793
- Yep, that's real.

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:45.847
- Right, and so that's the
conversation I'm having now

00:37:45.847 --> 00:37:49.240
and it's awesome.

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:50.073
It's awesome.

00:37:50.073 --> 00:37:55.060
And I think part of it is
because I say like I can't sing,

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:58.820
the Lord knew that if he
gave me a singing voice,

00:37:58.820 --> 00:38:00.210
I'd be out of control.

00:38:00.210 --> 00:38:03.390
